Over the past year or so several people (including me) have complained
about the tty version of the Windows port being unplayably slow. I was
just fooling around with the source code and figured out what is causing
this. Unfortunately, I'm not familiar enough with the NetHack source
code to write a patch to fix it. Maybe somebody out there will want to do
it... :-/

The problem is that the "high level" console API functions are being used
instead of the "low level" ones. In particular, WriteConsole() updates the
cursor position after outputting its text, and this is really slow. I
tried replacing all the calls to WriteConsole() in nttty.c with
WriteConsoleOutputCharacter() and the result was a blazing fast
exectuable. Of course, I also had to add calls to
WriteConsoleOutputAttribute().

The problem now is that the cursor position is wrong when the user is
supposed to provide input. The solution to this, of course, is to make
sure the cursor is moved to the right place whenver input is expected.
That's the part I am unable to do. It seems like tracking all the input
prompts in the source code would be a tedious project and not the best way
to go about it.

I hope this inspires somebody out there ;)

